new Pokemon in settings  that feel natural and make each of the  game's islands feel like individual  worlds and Sun and Moon also changes up  the flow of the game you're no longer on  quite the same quest of finding and  fighting eight gym leaders this time  you're a newcomer to an island paradise  with various trials you have to overcome  and for Kahuna trainers to fight to  prove that you're the best in the region  these trials add variety to the  otherwise straightforward battling  mechanics in one trial you have to  gather ingredients for a simple cooking  game while in another you take pictures  at Pokemon the trials are more varied in  the beginning of the game than at the  end but it's still a welcome break from  tradition  for each of the trials you earn a Z  crystal which unlocks super-powered  abilities for each Pokemon type but as  exciting as it sounds the crystals  ultimately feel like an unnecessary  addition much like the trials themselves  they become less involved further into  the game and the rewards feel a lot less  special when other characters just give  the crystals to you where you find them  scattered around the world and while the  animations look cool watching the same  long sequences every time you use the  move is just tiresome even with battle  animations turned off the powerful moves  the crystals unlocks still involve over  long summoning animations choosing  effective matchups against your  opponents is generally a better strategy  than relying on Z boobs  zyy moves are an addition you can ignore  but unfortunately Sun and moons biggest  weakness is something you can't the  story Sun and Moon starts out strong but  the antagonistic team skull enemies are  one-dimensional and directionless and  the motivations of the central  antagonist who's revealed in a twist you  see coming a mile away  are just nonsensical the final themes  shift from a confusing diatribe about  cleansing the world to your enemy hating  their children for not being beautiful  before coming to an abrupt and  unfulfilled conclusion but then none of  this narrative matters anyway as you're  pushed back onto the route of becoming a  pokémon champion and the core collecting  and battling gameplay is where sun and  moon shines anyway as is standard in  Pokemon games once the credits roll  there's still a whole world left to  explore and new Pokemon to catch  while the main narrative feels  unfinished Ultra beasts are glossed over  and it's unclear how exactly Legendary's  fit into a lola's world there's at least  the opportunity to catch these rare  beasts and see the effects of unleashing  a portal to another dimension on this  peaceful island world the biggest  argument for Sun and Moon is that  despite the story problems I want to  stay in a lola and see how the game's  mysteries unfold now that I'm the  region's champion for better and worse  Sun and Moon offers the same Pokemon  experience that comes out every few  years but this finally feels like a game  that goes beyond the hardcore fans for  players who have loved or been  interested in the franchise before but  who felt that the growing roster and  feature set made the series too  unapproachable Sun and Moon is a great  reintroduction to a long-lost friend
 

